\newcommand{\auc}{\text{AUC}}
\newcommand{\jsd}{\text{JSD}}

\title{Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text:\\Measuring the Discrimination Boundary}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

% === Abstract ===
\begin{abstract}
Web-scale corpora are increasingly contaminated by recursive synthetic content, yet no method estimates a document's generational depth.
We measure the discrimination boundary $\Kempirical$---the maximum depth at which adjacent generations remain distinguishable---using 15 statistical features and an OBD-LightGBM classifier.
Across 5 model configurations (1B--7B) and 4 decoding strategies, $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (Cohen's $h{\approx}0.20$), invariant across all 9 conditions: a single generation step is reliably detectable, but the second renders text statistically indistinguishable from deeper synthetic content---a \emph{one-wash laundering} effect.
Model-free statistical features enable 80--92\% cross-model transfer with scorer-invariant feature ordering ($\rho > 0.90$), and cross-scorer validation confirms that $\Kempirical$ is governed by the generation process rather than the scoring model.
Both supervised classification and zero-shot frontier LLMs fail comparably at depths ${\geq}2$ (recall 14--22\% vs.\ 8--38\%), confirming $\Kempirical{=}2$ as a property of the data rather than a method limitation.
Downstream, $\Kempirical{=}2$ correctly predicts that binary curation minimizes degradation most effectively among all tested strategies.
\end{abstract}

% === Section 1: Introduction ===
\input{introduction}

% === Section 2: Related Work ===
\input{related_work}

% === Section 3: Method ===
\input{method}

% === Section 4: Experiments ===
\input{experiments_c1}

% === Section 5: K*-Aware Data Filtering ===
\input{experiments_c3}

% === Section 6: Discussion ===
\input{discussion}

% === Section 7: Conclusion ===
\input{conclusion}

% === Limitations (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Limitations}

Our findings are bounded by (1)~model scale ($\leq$7B, with only one 7B configuration tested; the 7B condition uses Qwen2.5-7B-Base rather than Instruct, introducing a base-vs-instruct confound---though $\Kempirical{=}2$ consistency across this variation provides additional robustness evidence; cross-scale scoring rather than self-scoring), (2)~prompt-chain rather than retrain-chain protocols (our $\Kempirical$ is a conservative upper bound on retrain-chain $\Kstar$ under monotonic-degradation assumptions), (3)~English C4 text only, (4)~four decoding strategies, (5)~threshold sensitivity ($\Kempirical$ ranges from 2--3 at $\theta{=}0.55$ to 1 at $\theta{=}0.70$ depending on configuration; all five configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ at our chosen $\theta{=}0.60$), (6)~marginal boundary (Qwen-continuation 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.0015; cross-scorer validation with both Pythia and OLMo scorers drops this condition to $\Kempirical{=}1$, Section~\ref{sec:transfer}), (7)~the 15D feature set yields a feature-set-specific lower bound ($\Ktheory$ may be strictly higher), (8)~the neural probe boundary is model-dependent ($\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}{=}3$ for Qwen only; $2$ for Pythia/OLMo), likely reflecting self-familiarity rather than a universal signal, and (9)~while overall feature rankings are highly consistent across models ($\rho \geq 0.85$), depth-pair-level agreement weakens at the 1v2 boundary ($\tau \approx 0.37$), indicating that per-feature contributions at the critical boundary vary across models.
Detailed analysis appears in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.

% === Ethics Statement (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Ethics Statement}

Generational depth estimation is intended for data curation and corpus auditing---positive-sum applications that improve training data quality.
Our experiments use the publicly available C4 corpus and do not involve personally identifiable information.
A potential dual-use concern is that depth estimation techniques could inform adversarial strategies to evade detection (e.g., by calibrating rewriting to mimic earlier-generation statistics); we note that this risk is shared by all detection research and that the binary detection boundary ($\auc \geq 0.96$ at 0v1) remains robust.

